  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSurface low pressure over eastern Montana will rapidly lift \nnortheastward through western North Dakota and Manitoba today\nthrough tonight. An associated strong cold frontal boundary \nwill pass through the state from west to east this evening \nthrough tonight as well. Despite relatively low mixing heights,\nmoderate pressure rises and elevated lapse rates will aid in \ncreating very strong westerly winds. Winds of 25 to 35 mph are \nexpected with gusts up to 50 mph, especially in western North \nDakota. There is some uncertainty in regard to how strong winds \nwill be in the James River Valley as the pressure bubble weakens\nfurther eastward. However, confidence was high enough to issue \nthe Wind Advisory for all of the forecast area, except Dickey \nand LaMoure counties. Though even the far western portions of \nthose counties may see gusty winds along the downslope of the \nMissouri Coteau. Winds will then diminish from west to east late\ntonight and through the day Saturday; initially gradually, then\nrapidly.\n\nDue to expansive cloud cover during the first half of the day,\ntemperatures were relatively slow to rise. However, as skies\nclear from south to north, there has been a rapid increase.\nDickinson and Bismarck could still reach their record highs of\n79 and 77 degrees respectively. However, the peak heating window\nis beginning to run out as yesterday's highs were around 4 pm\nCDT. In the wake of the cold frontal boundary tonight, much\ncolder temperatures are expected through the next week with\nhighs mostly in the 40s and low 50s, with lows mostly in the \n20s and low 30s. \n\nIn regard to precipitation chances, a few showers and even a\ncouple rumbles of thunder are present in Foster County and may\ncontinue for the next hour or two. Some light rain is also\npossible in the far northwest tonight under low-level stratus\nbehind the front. Beyond that, expect mostly dry conditions\nthrough this weekend with some low rain chances near the ND/SD\nborder to start the next workweek. Low to medium rain chances\nare then in the forecast for the middle of next week.",
